From: ScorchDroid <noreply@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] android: show the game's own messages

Kills, joins and round notices go through ChannelManager::showText, whose
S3D_SERVER branch only writes to the Logger - upstream's client shows
them through ClientChannelManager in the other branch. A build that is
S3D_SERVER but still has a screen had nowhere to put them, so none of it
was ever seen. Reported against a real game: "[Player 1] killed [(Bot)
Jezebel] with a [Napalm]" shows in Scorched3D and nowhere here.

Parsed rather than logged raw. The message arrives as markup and
ChannelTextParser - which lives in common/common, so this needs nothing
from the client - resolves the player and weapon references into the
names they stand for.

Typed chat does not come through here (it goes through
ServerChannelManager::sendText, which engine_jni polls separately), so
nothing arrives twice.

No game logic changes.

diff --git a/src/common/common/ChannelManager.cpp b/src/common/common/ChannelManager.cpp
index d46665fa..765cf017 100644
--- a/src/common/common/ChannelManager.cpp
+++ b/src/common/common/ChannelManager.cpp
@@ -24,6 +24,12 @@
 	#include <client/ClientChannelManager.hpp>
 #else
 	#include <common/Logger.hpp>
+	// Android build: these lines are the game talking - kills, joins,
+	// round notices - and this port shows them in the same place it shows
+	// what players type. See ChatStore.h.
+	#include <ChatStore.h>
+	#include <common/ChannelTextParser.hpp>
+	#include <lang/LangString.hpp>
 #endif
 
 ChannelManager::ChannelManager()
@@ -40,5 +46,41 @@ void ChannelManager::showText(ScorchedContext &context, const ChannelText &text)
 #else
 	ChannelText &nonConst = (ChannelText &) text;
 	Logger::log(LangStringUtil::convertFromLang(nonConst.getMessage()));
+
+	// Android build: and on to the chat log, which is where a player can
+	// actually see it. Upstream's client shows these through its own
+	// ClientChannelManager in the branch above; a build that is S3D_SERVER
+	// but still has a screen had nowhere to put them, so every kill, every
+	// join and every round notice went to the log and no further.
+	//
+	// Parsed rather than logged raw: the message arrives as markup -
+	// "[p:{0}] killed [p:{1}] with a [w:{2}]" - and ChannelTextParser is
+	// what turns those references into the names and weapon they stand for.
+	// It lives in common/common, so this needs nothing from the client.
+	{
+		ChannelTextParser parser;
+		parser.parseText(context, nonConst.getMessage());
+
+		// ChannelTextParser marks each player reference with codepoint 3473
+		// (ChannelTextParser::createPlayerEntry), which upstream's own font
+		// draws as the little player icon before the name. Nothing here has
+		// that font, so it would arrive as a stray Sinhala letter or a tofu
+		// box in the middle of every kill message. The brackets around the
+		// name are kept - upstream shows those too.
+		LangString marked = parser.getString();
+		LangString clean;
+		for (size_t i = 0; i < marked.size(); i++)
+		{
+			if (marked[i] != 3473) clean.push_back(marked[i]);
+		}
+
+		ScorchDroidChat::Line line;
+		line.channel = nonConst.getChannel();
+		// No speaker: this is the game talking, not a player, which is what
+		// an empty name means downstream.
+		line.who = "";
+		line.text = LangStringUtil::convertFromLang(clean);
+		if (!line.text.empty()) ScorchDroidChat::push(line);
+	}
 #endif
 }
